\name{pva_process}
\alias{pva_process}
\alias{pva_result}
\alias{pva_events}
\alias{validate_processes}
\alias{execute_processes}
\title{Lifecycle processes and their execution contract}
\usage{
pva_process(
  name,
  run,
  params = list(),
  requires = character(),
  writes = character(),
  creates = NULL,
  description = NULL
)

pva_result(state, events = NULL, census = NULL)

pva_events(
  event_type,
  value = 1,
  label = NA_character_,
  origin_patch_id = NULL,
  destination_patch_id = NULL
)

validate_processes(processes, state = NULL)

execute_processes(
  state,
  processes,
  time = 0L,
  run = 1L,
  dt = 1,
  validation = c("strict", "fast")
)
}
\arguments{
\item{name}{Process name used in events, accounting and error messages.}
\item{run}{Function of \code{(state, context, params)} returning \code{pva_result()}.}
\item{params}{Named list of modeller parameters, passed unchanged to every supplied function as \code{params}.}
\item{requires}{Additional state paths (\code{population.<field>} or \code{globals.<name>}) read by the supplied functions; checked before a run starts.}
\item{writes}{State paths the process may change. Writing \code{population.alive} is how a process records deaths or removals.}
\item{creates}{For a process that adds individuals: the population fields it initialises. Must include \code{sex} and \code{age} and cover every non-core field.}
\item{description}{Optional one-line description.}
\item{state}{A \code{pva_state}.}
\item{events}{\code{NULL} or events from \code{pva_events()}.}
\item{census}{\code{NULL} or a named numeric vector of census metrics.}
\item{event_type}{Character vector of event types. \code{"death"}, \code{"removal"}, \code{"birth"} and \code{"introduction"} are accounting events whose values must match the state change.}
\item{value}{Numeric event values (length one, or one per event). Each accounting count must be a finite non-negative integer.}
\item{label}{Optional character labels such as a cause or source.}
\item{origin_patch_id}{Optional origin patch for each event, recorded as character.}
\item{destination_patch_id}{Optional destination patch for each event, supplied together with its origin.}
\item{processes}{Ordered list of \code{pva_process} objects.}
\item{time}{Zero-based interval index supplied in the process context.}
\item{dt}{Interval duration supplied in the process context.}
\item{validation}{\code{"strict"} checks every process transition (declared writes, types, engine-owned fields and cohort accounting). \code{"fast"} skips those checks and gives bit-identical output for a valid model.}
}
\description{
A lifecycle is a direct ordered list of ordinary R process objects. Each wraps a function and declares the state fields it requires, writes and creates. The engine calls the processes in order and owns time, identifiers, random-number streams, accounting and provenance.
}
\details{
The \code{context} passed to \code{run} is a read-only list with \code{run}, \code{time}, \code{dt}, \code{phase}, \code{n_steps}, \code{process_index}, \code{process_name}, \code{occurrence} and \code{departed_males} (IDs of males lost in earlier processes of this interval).

Under strict validation the engine checks after every process that only declared fields changed, that field types and lengths are intact, that existing records were neither deleted nor reordered, that new records came from \code{add_individuals()}, and that the accounting events equal the state change for each cohort separately: existing individuals that stopped living must equal the reported deaths or removals, and created individuals must equal the reported births or introductions. A process may report one loss type and one entry type per execution, and individuals it creates must be alive when it returns. Compensating errors that leave the net count unchanged are therefore still detected.

The engine records, for every process and interval, losses split into residents, individuals born in that interval and individuals introduced in that interval; see \code{\link{summarise_accounting}}.
}
\value{
\code{pva_process()} returns a validated \code{pva_process}; \code{pva_result()} a \code{pva_result}; \code{pva_events()} an event list. \code{validate_processes()} returns the validated list invisibly and, given a state, also checks the field plan. \code{execute_processes()} runs one interval with the caller's random-number stream and returns the new state with \code{events}, \code{census}, \code{accounting} and \code{ledger} data frames.
}
\examples{
harvest <- pva_process(
  name = "harvest_oldest",
  run = function(state, context, params) {
    oldest <- which.max(state$population$age)
    state$population$alive[oldest] <- FALSE
    pva_result(state, pva_events("removal", length(oldest), "harvest"))
  },
  requires = "population.age",
  writes = "population.alive"
)
state <- pva_state(pva_population(sex = c("F", "M", "F"), age = c(2, 9, 4)))
out <- execute_processes(state, list(harvest, census_process()))
out$census
out$accounting
}
